WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:01.320 --> 00:00:03.120
- All right, I think we found one.

00:00:03.120 --> 00:00:04.410
- [Narrator] This is Adam Lee.

00:00:04.410 --> 00:00:06.330
He's the chairman of Lee Auto Malls,

00:00:06.330 --> 00:00:08.640
which owns 16 dealerships in Maine.

00:00:08.640 --> 00:00:09.993
- That's used.

00:00:10.830 --> 00:00:12.234
Used.

00:00:12.234 --> 00:00:13.320
Used, used.

00:00:13.320 --> 00:00:14.580
- [Narrator] He's looking for a new car

00:00:14.580 --> 00:00:15.870
on their Toyota lot.

00:00:15.870 --> 00:00:17.670
- I don't know if we're
gonna find a new car

00:00:17.670 --> 00:00:20.610
but let's keep walking for a minute.

00:00:20.610 --> 00:00:22.800
- [Narrator] This is an
experience recent car shoppers

00:00:22.800 --> 00:00:24.480
are likely familiar with.

00:00:24.480 --> 00:00:26.310
Pandemic-related supply chain issues

00:00:26.310 --> 00:00:28.050
have pushed new vehicle inventory

00:00:28.050 --> 00:00:30.570
to historic lows, prompting higher prices

00:00:30.570 --> 00:00:32.970
and months-long waits for new cars.

00:00:32.970 --> 00:00:35.400
- People wanna buy more cars than we have,

00:00:35.400 --> 00:00:37.500
than we can possibly produce.

00:00:37.500 --> 00:00:38.970
This is not something

00:00:38.970 --> 00:00:42.210
that's happened in the
industry in decades.

00:00:42.210 --> 00:00:43.920
- [Narrator] The disruption,
coupled with the shift

00:00:43.920 --> 00:00:46.710
toward electric vehicles
is now causing consumers

00:00:46.710 --> 00:00:50.550
and automakers to rethink
how Americans buy cars.

00:00:50.550 --> 00:00:52.680
So let's take a walk around a dealership

00:00:52.680 --> 00:00:54.300
and through an EV showroom

00:00:54.300 --> 00:00:57.300
to see how the car purchasing
process has changed

00:00:57.300 --> 00:00:59.553
and what it might look like in the future.

00:01:01.080 --> 00:01:03.270
Buying a car has typically started here,

00:01:03.270 --> 00:01:05.340
at the dealership, but these days,

00:01:05.340 --> 00:01:06.990
there's not much to see.

00:01:06.990 --> 00:01:08.580
- If you'd come here
a couple of years ago,

00:01:08.580 --> 00:01:10.560
you just would have seen row after row,

00:01:10.560 --> 00:01:13.350
probably 10 to 15 rows of new inventory

00:01:13.350 --> 00:01:14.183
and some used

00:01:14.183 --> 00:01:17.850
but now what you've got is
a whole bunch of empty lot.

00:01:17.850 --> 00:01:19.140
- [Narrator] Nationally, there were about

00:01:19.140 --> 00:01:22.320
1.1 million new vehicles
available at the end of May.

00:01:22.320 --> 00:01:25.260
That's less than 1/3 of
what it was pre-pandemic.

00:01:25.260 --> 00:01:27.360
And inventory at Lee's dealerships

00:01:27.360 --> 00:01:29.010
is the lowest it's ever been.

00:01:29.010 --> 00:01:32.160
- I found one, I found
the one car we have,

00:01:32.160 --> 00:01:33.720
the one new car.

00:01:33.720 --> 00:01:35.670
- [Narrator] So rather
than searching the lot,

00:01:35.670 --> 00:01:38.160
many shoppers now search the internet.

00:01:38.160 --> 00:01:40.650
- We are seeing an increasing level

00:01:40.650 --> 00:01:44.040
of pre-car buying
activities happening online,

00:01:44.040 --> 00:01:46.650
whether that's researching
what kind of vehicle you need,

00:01:46.650 --> 00:01:48.330
whether that's reaching
out to the dealership

00:01:48.330 --> 00:01:49.680
and increasingly being able

00:01:49.680 --> 00:01:51.870
to see what inventory the dealership has.

00:01:51.870 --> 00:01:52.800
- [Narrator] At Lee's dealership,

00:01:52.800 --> 00:01:55.350
sales representatives field
these online inquiries,

00:01:55.350 --> 00:01:56.790
helping shoppers find cars

00:01:56.790 --> 00:01:58.350
and warning them about which vehicles

00:01:58.350 --> 00:02:01.860
may not be in stock for
weeks, even months to come.

00:02:01.860 --> 00:02:05.250
- In some cases, we have
a one-year waiting list.

00:02:05.250 --> 00:02:07.710
And so we sort of try to encourage them,

00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:09.180
if they're looking at a vehicle

00:02:09.180 --> 00:02:11.550
that's gonna take a year to arrive

00:02:11.550 --> 00:02:13.590
to look at alternatives.

00:02:13.590 --> 00:02:14.760
And a lot of times,

00:02:14.760 --> 00:02:17.430
people they know what
they want, they'll wait.

00:02:17.430 --> 00:02:19.050
- [Narrator] Waiting
means getting in line.

00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:22.140
And getting in line means putting
down a refundable deposit.

00:02:22.140 --> 00:02:23.130
- Shem, how are you?
- Good.

00:02:23.130 --> 00:02:24.660
- [Narrator] At Lee's Toyota dealership,

00:02:24.660 --> 00:02:27.830
around 300 deposits
are still waiting cars.

00:02:27.830 --> 00:02:30.750
- On the one hand, it's
nice to have 300 sold orders

00:02:30.750 --> 00:02:31.583
in the bank.

00:02:31.583 --> 00:02:32.910
When the cars come in, they go out.

00:02:32.910 --> 00:02:34.920
On the other hand, in the old days,

00:02:34.920 --> 00:02:36.870
those cars would have already
been sitting on the lot

00:02:36.870 --> 00:02:38.160
and we would have delivered them already.

00:02:38.160 --> 00:02:40.740
But everyone seems to
have adapted pretty well.

00:02:40.740 --> 00:02:42.300
- [Narrator] Analysts say leaner inventory

00:02:42.300 --> 00:02:44.280
has actually benefited many dealers

00:02:44.280 --> 00:02:46.560
as the expense to carry
a vehicle on the lot

00:02:46.560 --> 00:02:48.300
is greatly reduced.

00:02:48.300 --> 00:02:50.130
- The less time you keep a vehicle

00:02:50.130 --> 00:02:51.150
on your dealership lot,

00:02:51.150 --> 00:02:52.350
and the faster that you can sell it,

00:02:52.350 --> 00:02:54.360
it is going to be a more profitable sale.

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:56.460
- [Narrator] Overall sales
volume at Lee's dealerships

00:02:56.460 --> 00:03:00.120
are down roughly 10 to 15%
from pre-pandemic levels

00:03:00.120 --> 00:03:03.180
but he says the business,
like many other dealerships,

00:03:03.180 --> 00:03:05.490
is still quite profitable.

00:03:05.490 --> 00:03:06.690
A big reason?

00:03:06.690 --> 00:03:08.070
The cost of cars.

00:03:08.070 --> 00:03:11.580
- I happen to have the MSRP sticker

00:03:11.580 --> 00:03:13.500
to the one new car we have in the lot.

00:03:13.500 --> 00:03:14.700
- [Narrator] That price Lee is showing

00:03:14.700 --> 00:03:18.900
is the manufacturer's
suggested retail price or MSRP.

00:03:18.900 --> 00:03:20.760
Suggested is the keyword here

00:03:20.760 --> 00:03:22.140
because dealers are the ones

00:03:22.140 --> 00:03:24.090
who ultimately price the vehicle.

00:03:24.090 --> 00:03:27.150
So this car is 45,109.

00:03:27.150 --> 00:03:28.710
- [Narrator] Typically,
buyers would negotiate

00:03:28.710 --> 00:03:32.730
that price they see in the
window down even further but now.

00:03:32.730 --> 00:03:34.980
- I don't think there's an awful
lot of negotiating going on

00:03:34.980 --> 00:03:36.450
on the price of the new car,

00:03:36.450 --> 00:03:38.670
at least not at our stores.

00:03:38.670 --> 00:03:40.140
- [Narrator] With few
cars and high demand,

00:03:40.140 --> 00:03:42.840
shoppers don't have
much power to negotiate.

00:03:42.840 --> 00:03:45.240
But dealers and analysts say negotiations

00:03:45.240 --> 00:03:47.580
were trending out even
before the pandemic.

00:03:47.580 --> 00:03:50.040
- Price negotiation is a scenario

00:03:50.040 --> 00:03:52.080
that consumers love to hate.

00:03:52.080 --> 00:03:52.913
Hate to love.

00:03:52.913 --> 00:03:53.746
(Stephanie chuckling)

00:03:53.746 --> 00:03:54.990
And they want to be able

00:03:54.990 --> 00:03:57.780
to feel like they've gotten
a bit of a deal on something

00:03:57.780 --> 00:04:00.000
but they don't like the process of it.

00:04:00.000 --> 00:04:01.680
- [Narrator] Even without
negotiation though.

00:04:01.680 --> 00:04:04.140
Shoppers could typically
expect to pay less,

00:04:04.140 --> 00:04:08.010
thanks to deals and rebates
that push prices below MSRP.

00:04:08.010 --> 00:04:09.990
But incentive levels on new vehicles

00:04:09.990 --> 00:04:11.910
hit historic lows in May,

00:04:11.910 --> 00:04:15.660
averaging 2.5% of the
average transaction price.

00:04:15.660 --> 00:04:18.300
- Right now, dealers
don't have to offer them.

00:04:18.300 --> 00:04:20.520
When they're going to
have to offer incentives

00:04:20.520 --> 00:04:22.500
and when this negotiation sort

00:04:22.500 --> 00:04:25.920
of starts to resume will depend
on inventory coming back.

00:04:25.920 --> 00:04:26.970
- [Narrator] And that could take more

00:04:26.970 --> 00:04:28.620
than a year analysts say,

00:04:28.620 --> 00:04:31.710
meaning prices are likely to remain high.

00:04:31.710 --> 00:04:33.390
So far this year, average prices

00:04:33.390 --> 00:04:35.670
have been over MSRP each month.

00:04:35.670 --> 00:04:39.540
In May, buyers paid an average
of about $900 over sticker.

00:04:39.540 --> 00:04:42.750
For in-demand models, markups
can be in the thousands.

00:04:42.750 --> 00:04:45.300
- We have always chosen not
to mark up a hot vehicle.

00:04:45.300 --> 00:04:48.870
Just that we feel the sticker
price, it's reasonable.

00:04:48.870 --> 00:04:50.460
Three years ago, we
would have been thrilled

00:04:50.460 --> 00:04:52.680
if you said you're gonna
sell every car at MSRP.

00:04:52.680 --> 00:04:54.030
We would have said, "That's great."

00:04:54.030 --> 00:04:55.620
- [Narrator] Manufacturers
have warned dealers

00:04:55.620 --> 00:04:57.420
against markups, saying they hurt

00:04:57.420 --> 00:04:59.310
the brand's image with consumers.

00:04:59.310 --> 00:05:02.040
But again, dealers set the price,

00:05:02.040 --> 00:05:03.993
at least for legacy automakers.

00:05:05.040 --> 00:05:06.690
Startup electric vehicle companies,

00:05:06.690 --> 00:05:09.450
like Lucid Motors take
a different approach,

00:05:09.450 --> 00:05:11.460
selling their cars primarily online,

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:13.110
directly to consumers.

00:05:13.110 --> 00:05:14.610
- Oftentimes, they know what they want

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:17.940
and in that case, having
a digital experience

00:05:17.940 --> 00:05:19.981
that is directly connected to the company

00:05:19.981 --> 00:05:23.520
that's producing the
vehicles just simplifies it.

00:05:23.520 --> 00:05:25.530
- [Narrator] There is an
offline component here.

00:05:25.530 --> 00:05:27.390
Lucid, like Tesla and Rivian

00:05:27.390 --> 00:05:29.730
have showrooms where customers can browse,

00:05:29.730 --> 00:05:32.130
sit in and customize a car in VR

00:05:32.130 --> 00:05:33.930
before they decide to buy.

00:05:33.930 --> 00:05:37.020
But when they do, the price is the price.

00:05:37.020 --> 00:05:39.540
No negotiation, no incentives.

00:05:39.540 --> 00:05:42.030
- I think it allows both sides,

00:05:42.030 --> 00:05:44.010
both Lucid and the customer

00:05:44.010 --> 00:05:46.590
to really focus on
learning and experience,

00:05:46.590 --> 00:05:50.040
rather than this
confrontational negotiation

00:05:50.040 --> 00:05:51.630
that typically takes place.

00:05:51.630 --> 00:05:53.370
- [Narrator] But selling
from a studio like this

00:05:53.370 --> 00:05:54.840
isn't legal everywhere.

00:05:54.840 --> 00:05:56.880
State franchise laws require vehicles

00:05:56.880 --> 00:06:00.000
in most places to be priced
and sold by a dealer.

00:06:00.000 --> 00:06:02.730
- Ideally, there wouldn't be any laws

00:06:02.730 --> 00:06:05.010
that say that a manufacturer of products

00:06:05.010 --> 00:06:07.080
can't sell them directly
to their customer.

00:06:07.080 --> 00:06:09.960
So yeah, we're actually
working to find ways

00:06:09.960 --> 00:06:11.763
to overturn those rules.

00:06:12.779 --> 00:06:14.520
- [Narrator] While it's only EV makers

00:06:14.520 --> 00:06:16.290
using the direct sales model now,

00:06:16.290 --> 00:06:18.990
legacy automakers are
eyeing a similar approach

00:06:18.990 --> 00:06:20.580
as they shift electric.

00:06:20.580 --> 00:06:23.280
Ford, in particular,
plans to release new rules

00:06:23.280 --> 00:06:25.530
for how it sells its EVs in September.

00:06:25.530 --> 00:06:27.330
CEO Jim Farley spoke last month

00:06:27.330 --> 00:06:29.760
about keeping zero inventory at dealers

00:06:29.760 --> 00:06:32.280
and sales being 100% online.

00:06:32.280 --> 00:06:33.540
- [James] I think our dealers can do it

00:06:33.540 --> 00:06:35.970
but the standards are gonna be brutal.

00:06:35.970 --> 00:06:37.830
They're gonna be very
different than today.

00:06:37.830 --> 00:06:39.090
- [Narrator] Some dealers are concerned

00:06:39.090 --> 00:06:41.280
this may be an attempt to cut them out.

00:06:41.280 --> 00:06:43.650
Farley recently met
with around 300 dealers

00:06:43.650 --> 00:06:45.630
to discuss concerns about EV sales.

00:06:45.630 --> 00:06:49.110
He emphasized that dealers
are key to Ford's success.

00:06:49.110 --> 00:06:52.410
Lee, who doesn't sell
Fords, sees a hybrid model.

00:06:52.410 --> 00:06:54.750
- Maybe you order it online
from the manufacturer

00:06:54.750 --> 00:06:56.640
but I believe it's gonna, for most,

00:06:56.640 --> 00:06:58.530
it will go to the dealership,

00:06:58.530 --> 00:07:00.180
and they'll deliver the car to you.

00:07:00.180 --> 00:07:01.800
- [Narrator] This
possible shift in approach

00:07:01.800 --> 00:07:04.290
toward EV sales comes as manufacturers

00:07:04.290 --> 00:07:05.820
and dealers alike are learning

00:07:05.820 --> 00:07:08.250
from the changes over the last two years.

00:07:08.250 --> 00:07:12.090
- There were seeds for
a potential for changes

00:07:12.090 --> 00:07:16.320
in a process that wasn't always
comfortable for consumers,

00:07:16.320 --> 00:07:17.580
and those seeds were there

00:07:17.580 --> 00:07:20.040
and they are being explored,
and they've been accelerated

00:07:20.040 --> 00:07:22.980
by a number of things
by Tesla, by challengers

00:07:22.980 --> 00:07:26.790
and certainly by macro
and external events.

00:07:26.790 --> 00:07:29.190
- [Narrator] Question
is what's here to stay?

00:07:29.190 --> 00:07:31.950
Analysts and dealers expect
more online activities

00:07:31.950 --> 00:07:34.050
and less negotiation in the future

00:07:34.050 --> 00:07:37.710
but say incentives will
return as inventory does,

00:07:37.710 --> 00:07:40.350
even if it doesn't go
back to where it was.

00:07:40.350 --> 00:07:43.380
- I think lower inventory
levels may stick.

00:07:43.380 --> 00:07:44.213
I hope they do.

00:07:44.213 --> 00:07:45.046
It would be better

00:07:45.046 --> 00:07:48.300
if we had 100 new cars
sitting here, not 300.

00:07:48.300 --> 00:07:49.530
We don't need that many.

00:07:49.530 --> 00:07:51.660
- [Narrator] Still, it's not an empty lot

00:07:51.660 --> 00:07:54.210
or even a full one that worries Lee.

00:07:54.210 --> 00:07:56.970
Instead, he's concerned
about rising interest rates

00:07:56.970 --> 00:07:58.560
and a possible recession.

00:07:58.560 --> 00:08:00.870
- I think some of these
changes might help dealers

00:08:00.870 --> 00:08:02.160
weather the next recession

00:08:02.160 --> 00:08:04.080
but in the end, if people
aren't buying cars,

00:08:04.080 --> 00:08:05.065
you're not making money.

00:08:05.065 --> 00:08:07.732
(pensive music)

